uated (either through clinical-attribution language like \"presenting with\" or anchored by a proper name/gene/pathogen identifier), then it should be labeled SpecificDisease rather than DiseaseClass. I'm also adding a negative constraint so umbrella terms in non-specific contexts like \"family history of cancer\" stay DiseaseClass, and clarifying that list position or presentation context alone shouldn't drive the decision—it has to come from referential scope. I'll keep the whole thing concise and finalize it as one principle.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Referential Scope Over Head-Noun Genericity:** IF a disease mention's head noun is a superordinate category term (e.g., a generic class of pathology, infection, or deficiency), AND the mention is referentially individuated, meaning either (a) it is attributed to a specific patient or case through a clinical-attribution construction (e.g., a participial or prepositional predicate such as \"presenting with,\" \"found to have,\" or \"diagnosed with\"), or (b) it is anchored by a proper name, eponym, acronym, or a gene, enzyme, or pathogen identifier serving as a premodifier or postmodifier, THEN label the mention **SpecificDisease**.\n  - **Negative Constraint:** Do NOT apply this rule when the category term is an unindividuated umbrella that quantifies over multiple disorder types, with no patient attribution and no anchoring identifier (e.g., a bare generic noun inside a non-specific history or epidemiological statement, such as \"a family history of [category]\"). Those mentions remain **DiseaseClass**. Also, do not treat the head noun's generality, its position in a list, or the surrounding clinical context as sufficient evidence on their own. Decide by referential scope.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_t